   $q$Read the sentence: "The volleyball team either will practice outdoors or in the gym across town." Which revision places "either" and "or" so that the sentence is parallel?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Either the volleyball team will practice outdoors or in the gym across town.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"correlative-misplaced"},{"text":"The volleyball team will practice either outdoors or in the gym across town.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The volleyball team will either practice outdoors or in the gym across town.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"correlative-misplaced"},{"text":"The volleyball team either will practice outdoors or will in the gym across town.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"correlative-misplaced"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The volleyball team will practice either outdoors or in the gym across town.$q$, $q$The two options are places, "outdoors" and "in the gym across town," so "either" belongs right before "outdoors." Placing "either" before "practice" pairs a verb with a prepositional phrase, which is not parallel.$q$, 'draft', null)
