        '9-12', $q$Tiny Rings, Long Journeys$q$, $q$(1) Every spring at the Millbrook Nature Center, volunteers stretch fine nets between trees to catch small songbirds for a few minutes. (2) They are not keeping the birds. (3) Instead, they are taking part in bird banding.

(4) A bird band is a lightweight metal ring printed with a unique number. (5) A trained bander gently fastens the ring around one of the bird's legs, records its species, age, weight and wing length, and then releases it. (6) The whole process usually takes less than two minutes. (7) My cousin once saw a bald eagle sitting in a tree at a state park.

(8) The real value of banding comes later. (9) If someone captures or finds the same bird again, the number on its band connects the two records. (10) In 2019, a warbler banded at Millbrook was recaptured in a forest more than 3,000 kilometers to the south, showing the route it had flown for the winter. (11) Records like this one help scientists map migration paths and learn how long different species survive.

(12) Banding reveals changes over time. (13) Between 2005 and 2020, Millbrook volunteers banded about 1,800 wood thrushes, but the number captured each year dropped by nearly half. (14) That decline encouraged the center to protect more of the forest where the thrushes nest.

(15) That is everything about bird banding.$q$, 'draft')
