   $q$A classmate says the claim in sentence 2 is not supported because the draft only lists facts about a train. Which response best defends the draft?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Sentences 3 through 5 show how cut off the towns were, and sentences 7 through 10 show the car bringing outside news and inspiring new libraries.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the dates 1921 to 1934, which proves that the reading car was important.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weak-evidence"},{"text":"Sentence 12 repeats the claim, and repeating a claim is enough to prove that it is true.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"repetition-as-proof"},{"text":"The claim does not need support, because historians like Kowalczyk are always correct about the past.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"appeal-to-authority"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Sentences 3 through 5 show how cut off the towns were, and sentences 7 through 10 show the car bringing outside news and inspiring new libraries.$q$, $q$The claim says the car connected isolated towns to the world, and the draft shows both the isolation and the connection with specific evidence. The dates in sentence 1 only tell when the car ran, not what it accomplished.$q$, 'draft', '8effa8cb-b877-53d5-b31a-3ceef8d9f4ab')
