   $q$Why is it effective that the writer limits the claim in sentence (3) to the largest hemlocks along the streams rather than every hemlock in the forest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The passage states that the commission has already treated all the other hemlocks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"invents-fact"},{"text":"The evidence centers on shade for trout streams, so a narrower claim matches the evidence and keeps costs lower.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The writer believes the hemlocks away from the streams are not worth saving at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overreads-omission"},{"text":"The passage states that the insect attacks only trees growing near water.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The evidence centers on shade for trout streams, so a narrower claim matches the evidence and keeps costs lower.$q$, $q$Sentences (1), (4) and (5) all focus on streamside trees and trout, so a precise claim about those trees fits the evidence. The passage never says the insect attacks only trees near water, so that choice relies on information not given.$q$, 'draft', 'df3f57e2-4c90-55ba-8db2-54649a4d7cd1')
