   $q$Read the sentence: "To make the glaze for the ceramics project, students needed: silica, feldspar, and a small amount of cobalt." Which revision corrects the punctuation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To make the glaze for the ceramics project, students needed: silica; feldspar; and a small amount of cobalt.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-verb"},{"text":"To make the glaze for the ceramics project, students needed; silica, feldspar, and a small amount of cobalt.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-for-colon"},{"text":"To make the glaze for the ceramics project, students needed three ingredients: silica, feldspar, and a small amount of cobalt.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To make the glaze for the ceramics project: students needed silica, feldspar, and a small amount of cobalt.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-phrase"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To make the glaze for the ceramics project, students needed three ingredients: silica, feldspar, and a small amount of cobalt.$q$, $q$Adding "three ingredients" turns the words before the colon into a complete clause, so the colon can properly introduce the list. Simply swapping in a semicolon does not help, because the list is not an independent clause.$q$, 'draft', null)
