   $q$Which sentence about a history exhibit uses a colon correctly before a quotation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The placard summed up the exhibit in one line: \"Every trade route carried ideas as well as goods.\"","is_correct":true},{"text":"According to the placard: \"Every trade route carried ideas as well as goods.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-phrase"},{"text":"The placard said that: \"Every trade route carried ideas as well as goods.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-that"},{"text":"The placard explained: that \"every trade route carried ideas as well as goods.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-before-that"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The placard summed up the exhibit in one line: "Every trade route carried ideas as well as goods."$q$, $q$Only "The placard summed up the exhibit in one line" puts a complete independent clause before the colon, which is what a colon introducing a quotation requires. "According to the placard" is just a phrase, and "said that" leads directly into the quoted words, so neither takes a colon.$q$, 'draft', null)
